From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Lamprecht <t.lamprecht@proxmox.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 14:55:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PVE backup: vma reader: avoid bounce buffers for direct I/O
 restores

With cache=none, as qemu-server uses for restores to ZFS pools, QEMU
opens the target with O_DIRECT. Zvols and device-mapper volumes, like
LVM-thin ones, need memory aligned to 512 bytes for that. The extent
buffer holding the restore data had no such alignment, so QEMU copied
every write into an aligned bounce buffer first.

Align the buffer, and the block used for writing zeroes, so that the
data can be written directly.

Restoring an 8 GiB disk from a zstd-compressed archive like qemu-server
does, onto a zvol on NVMe with cache=none, took on average 4.5s instead
of 5.9s. For an LVM-thin volume, where the disk was the limit, the
difference stayed within the noise.

Signed-off-by: Thomas Lamprecht <t.lamprecht@proxmox.com>
Acked-by: Wolfgang Bumiller <w.bumiller@proxmox.com>
---
 vma-reader.c | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/vma-reader.c b/vma-reader.c
index 92f48ee0cd..1fb6223a5c 100644
--- a/vma-reader.c
+++ b/vma-reader.c
@@ -21,7 +21,8 @@
 #include "block/block.h"
 #include "system/block-backend.h"
 
-static unsigned char zero_vma_block[VMA_BLOCK_SIZE];
+/* aligned like the extent buffer in vma_reader_restore_full */
+static unsigned char zero_vma_block[VMA_BLOCK_SIZE] QEMU_ALIGNED(512);
 
 typedef struct VmaRestoreState {
     BlockBackend *target;
@@ -791,7 +792,12 @@ static int vma_reader_restore_full(VmaReader *vmar, int vmstate_fd,
     assert(vmar->head_data);
 
     int ret = 0;
-    unsigned char buf[VMA_MAX_EXTENT_SIZE];
+    /*
+     * Direct I/O to targets like zvols or device-mapper volumes needs memory
+     * aligned to 512 bytes, otherwise QEMU copies every write to a bounce
+     * buffer first. As the extent header is 512 bytes, this aligns the data.
+     */
+    unsigned char buf[VMA_MAX_EXTENT_SIZE] QEMU_ALIGNED(512);
     int buf_pos = 0;
     unsigned char md5sum[16];
     VmaHeader *h = (VmaHeader *)vmar->head_data;
